      title: "Sargon of Akkad",
      body: `## A foundling king

Around **2334 BC** (by the most commonly used chronology, which may be off by several decades), a man named **Sargon** created the first empire in history. His name, in Akkadian *Šarru-kīn*, means "the true king" or "the legitimate king"—probably a throne name chosen by a man whose claim to the throne was anything but legitimate.

Centuries after his death, the Assyrians told a story of his birth. "My mother was a high priestess," Sargon says in the text known as the **Sargon Legend**; "my father I did not know. … My mother conceived me and bore me in secret. She set me in a basket of rushes, sealed its lid with bitumen, and cast me into the river, which did not rise over me. The river carried me to Aqqi, the drawer of water," who raised him as his own son and made him a gardener. While he was a gardener, the goddess Ishtar loved him, and he became king. The story is legend, written down perhaps fifteen hundred years later, but its resemblance to the story of the infant **Moses**, placed in a basket of bulrushes daubed with pitch and set among the reeds of the Nile (Exodus 2:3), has been noted since it was first translated.

According to the Sumerian King List and other traditions, Sargon began his career as the **cupbearer** of **Ur-Zababa**, the king of **Kish**, a city in northern Babylonia. How he rose to power is unknown; later legends tell of a dream and a plot against his life. He founded, or refounded, a city called **Akkad** (or Agade), which became the capital of his empire and gave its name to the **Akkadian** language and to the whole region of northern Babylonia. The site of Akkad has never been found; it probably lies somewhere near modern Baghdad, buried beneath later settlements or the shifting course of the rivers.

## Conquest

Sargon's great achievement was to unite all of southern Mesopotamia under a single ruler for the first time, and to extend his power far beyond it. He defeated **Lugalzagesi** of Uruk, who had united much of Sumer, captured him, and, according to his inscriptions, brought him to Nippur in a neck stock, to the gate of Enlil's temple. He then conquered the cities of Sumer one by one, tore down their walls, and "**washed his weapons in the sea**"—the Persian Gulf, which the Mesopotamians called the "Lower Sea." He campaigned northwest along the Euphrates into Syria, as far as the "**Cedar Forest**" and the "**Silver Mountains**" (the Amanus and Taurus ranges of southern Turkey), and east into **Elam**, in southwestern Iran.

Sargon's inscriptions, preserved in later copies made by scribes at Nippur, boast that ships from **Meluhha** (the Indus Valley), **Magan** (Oman), and **Dilmun** (Bahrain) docked at the quay of Akkad. They also boast that "**5,400 men ate daily before him**"—a standing army, or a large body of royal dependents, supported by the king, the first such force in history. Sargon reigned, according to tradition, for fifty-six years.

## A new kind of state

The Akkadian empire was something new. Earlier rulers had dominated other cities; Sargon and his successors tried to govern them. They replaced the local rulers of the Sumerian cities with their own governors, often Akkadians, garrisoned their soldiers in conquered cities, imposed a standard system of weights and measures, and used **Akkadian**, a Semitic language, alongside Sumerian in official documents. They seized land from the temples and the local elites and gave it to their own followers. And they placed their own relatives in important religious offices, as we will see with Sargon's daughter.

The empire rested on force, and it faced constant rebellion. Sargon's sons and successors, **Rimush** and **Manishtushu**, had to reconquer the Sumerian cities repeatedly; their inscriptions record tens of thousands of enemies killed or captured. Both were apparently murdered by their own courtiers.

## Akkadian

The Akkadian language that Sargon's empire spread would become the most important language of the ancient Near East for two thousand years. Its later dialects, **Babylonian** and **Assyrian**, were the languages of the great empires that followed, and Akkadian written in cuneiform became, in the second millennium BC, the international language of diplomacy, used from Egypt to Anatolia. Akkadian is the oldest recorded Semitic language, related to Hebrew, Aramaic, and Arabic.

## The memory of Sargon

Sargon became the model of the great conqueror for all later Mesopotamian kings. Two Assyrian kings, more than fifteen hundred years later, took his name: **Sargon I** and **Sargon II**. Legends, epics, and omens grew up around him, portraying him as the ideal ruler, beloved of the gods, who traveled to the ends of the earth. One text, the "**Sargon Geography**," describes the extent of his empire; another, "**The King of Battle**," tells how he marched to Anatolia to protect Akkadian merchants. Later kings went to great lengths to find and copy his inscriptions. The Neo-Babylonian king Nabonidus, as we will see at the end of this course, excavated temples in search of inscriptions of Sargon's dynasty.

## Chronology

A word about dates. The dates of Mesopotamian history in the third and second millennia BC are uncertain. They are calculated from lists of kings and their reigns, and anchored by a few astronomical observations, especially observations of the planet Venus recorded in the reign of a king of Babylon in the seventeenth century BC. But these observations can fit several different dates, producing several possible chronologies—"high," "middle," and "low"—which differ by up to a century or more. This course uses the "**middle chronology**," which is the most widely used, but readers should remember that the dates before about 1400 BC are approximate. Recent studies using tree rings and radiocarbon dating have tended to support the middle chronology or a slightly lower one.

> **Pause and consider.** Sargon's legend has him set adrift in a basket of reeds as an infant. Why might such a story be told about a usurper? What does the parallel with Moses suggest?`,
